I've just put ttysnoop 0.12 on sunsite. This version has been around
for a while but I noticed nobody had it yet, so here it is. :)

This version has one new feature. It allows you to suspend/resume
snooping with a single keypress.

Below are the README and brandnew FAQ-list.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

README for ttysnoop 0.12 (alpha) -- 06-09-94

This is version 0.12 of ttysnoop for Linux. It introduces one new
feature and now also has support for shadow passwords. (Thanx to
those who mailed me the patches)

The package allows you to snoop on login tty's through another
tty-device or pseudo-tty. The snoop-tty becomes a 'clone' of the
original tty, redirecting both input and output from/to it.

To install, untar the archive, customize Makefile and config.h and
type 'make'. If all goes well, type 'make install' next and copy
the example snooptab.dist file to /etc/snooptab. Edit this new copy
and tell your getty/telnetd to execute /sbin/ttysnoops as the login
program (instead of /bin/login). You should then be able to snoop
upon the tty's listed in /etc/snooptab whevener a user logs in on
them.

Mail any bug-reports/patches/comments to carl@miskatonic.nfe.be or
carl@p10.f500.n292.z2.fidonet.org.


History log:

>From v0.11 onwards, user authentication is performed via a password
prompt whenever snooping is done through a pty.

>From v0.12 onwards, the ttysnoop client supports a suspend/resume
key (default ctrl-\) which suspends/resumes snooping when it is
pressed. Might come in handy when snooping rz/sz sessions. ;)


    +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
    | Smallish FAQ-list for the ttysnoop package. Last update 19-11-94. |
    +-------------------------------------------------------------------+

    I started putting this together after receiving lots of similar 
    questions on ttysnoop and how to set it up.

    Here goes...


Q : What is ttysnoop?

A : Good question. It's a little tool that allows system administrators
    (or any other authorised persons) to watch, spy, snoop or
    whatever you want to call it, on user-logins. These logins can
    be either through a dial-in line (modem) or from the network
    (telnet session).

	Technically, this is what it actually does:

    A 'server', called ttysnoops, is run right after getty or
    telnetd has accepted the user's login name, but usually even
    before /bin/login is executed. The server creates a pseudo-tty
    and runs any subsequent processes in the login-chain as slaves
    to this pty. The server then continually passes any slave IO
    from/to the other end of the connection (ie. the unsuspecting
    user :) as well as to a snoop-device (ie. the snooping sysadmin).


Q : There's a ttysnoops binary and a ttysnoop binary. What's the
    difference?

A : ttysnoops with an s is the server. It is always used when
    snooping on a user. See the technical answer to question 1.
    The other one, ttysnoop without an s, is the client. It is only
    needed by the snooper when snooping through a socket (see
    further). It accepts one argument and that is the leafname (as
    reported by the commands 'w' or 'who') of the pseudo-tty to
    snoop.


Q : In the docs you say "tell your getty/telnetd to start
    /sbin/ttysnoops instead of /bin/login." How do I do that?

A : This is by far the most frequently asked question. Let's start
    with getty. If you're using getty_ps, like I am, go to the
    /etc/default directory and edit the file(s) uugetty.ttySx,
    where x is the logical com-port number of your dial-in line(s).
    Put the line

		LOGIN=/sbin/ttysnoops

	somewhere at the top. This 'tells' uugetty to execute the
	ttysnoops server instead of the default /bin/login.

	If you're using mgetty, edit the file called login.config,
	which is probably in /usr/local/lib/mgetty and add the
    following line:

		*       -       -       /sbin/ttysnoops @

	This will make mgetty start the ttysnoops server when a
	user logs in.  Read the mgetty documentation to find out
    what exactly is happening.

    Telling telnetd how to start ttysnoops is a bit trickier and
    probably depends on what 'flavour' of telnetd you're running.
    The ones I've seen don't have these nice runtime config files
    and should be modified at the source code level. So grab your
    telnetd's sources and look for lines that have got the string
    "/bin/login" in them. Change all of these into "/sbin/ttysnoops"
    and recompile & install the new telnetd.

	An alternative way to get snooped logins is to rename the
	existing /bin/login to something like /bin/login.snooped
    and rename /sbin/ttysnoops to /bin/login. If you choose to do
    it like this, change all occurences of /bin/login in /etc/snooptab
    to /bin/login.snooped.  This method may be a bit easier to
    install but it will snoop *ALL* logins, including those from
    virtual consoles, which is something you may not want to do.


Q : How do I set up /etc/snooptab? I'm confused.

A : It's all fairly simple really. The snooptab file is used by
    ttysnoops so that it knows how to snoop on logins on a specific
    dial-in line (or telnet connection). The first field on each
    line tells us the name of a line we want to snoop, for example
    ttyS3 (don't include the /dev/ prefix). The next field tells
    us 'where' (or how) we want to snoop on this line. Just give
    the full pathname of a device (or even file) through which you
    want to see what the user is doing. This is usually an unused
    virtual console, like /dev/tty7 or /dev/tty8, but could just
    as well be another serial line, a file, a printer or whatever
    as long as it's a character device. There is an exeption to
    this and that is if you don't want to permanently snoop on a
    user but only, say, if you really think he's being naughty.
    This is when you want to snoop 'through a socket' with the
    ttysnoop client. In this case, put the literal string "socket"
    in the second field.  The third field is currently not really
    important and is best left as "login". The last field is the
    program that should be executed once the ttysnoop server has
    begun snooping this login, and usually is the standard login
    program /bin/login (or /bin/login.snooped if you chose for the
    alternative ttysnoop installation).
    That's it basically. Include an entry for each dial-in line
    you want to snoop. But what if a user logs in from the network?
    In that case, the login 'line' will be a pseudo-tty (pty) and
    will be called something like ttyp4. Because you generally
    don't know beforehand which pty will be allocated during a
    network login, you can put the '*' wildcard as the *LAST* entry
    in your snooptab and it will match any login 'line'. Note that
    this last entry also better had a "socket" snoop device since
    LOTS of network logins might occur and couldn't all be snooped
    simultaneously through, say, /dev/tty8.
    On lots of systems, the only entry in snooptab is just the
    wildcard-line.


Q : Ttysnoop works ok, but sometimes after users have logged out,
    they are still visible with 'w' or 'who'. What's up?

A : I don't know. Some people have reported this and it does seem
    to be a ttysnoop related problem, but unless you give me the
    exact details of your setup, ie. getty package & version,
    ttysnoop version, dial-in or network login, etc... I cannot
    track down the problem. I *DO* know there is/was a problem with
    older versions of getty_ps because I had it myself but it
    suddenly disappeared after upgrading various programs. So if
    you have the problem and it is persistent, even after upgrading
    to the most current versions of all programs involved, send me
    a _detailed_ problem report so I can investigate the problem.


Q : I adore ttysnoop, I also want to use it on my $100000000 Cray
    thingy. Has it been ported to any other platforms?

A : Lots of people seem to ask this. I can't really help unless I
    have access to such a platform. Currently the only other-than-Linux
    version is a BSDI one, which still has a nasty bug that needs
    to be fixed. I would love to receive patches with support for
    other systems so I can include them in the main archive. If
    you have done any porting, please send me the patches so that
    other people may benefit from your work.


Q : What's the current version of ttysnoop?

A : At the time of writing, it's v0.12.


                             --- ooo OOO ooo ---

