I am running a Linux FTP site on linux.nrao.edu (192.33.115.50), a 90MHz
Pentium running Linux.  It currently carries a full Slackware mirror
(sans the source tree for now) from ftp.cdrom.com, as well as a mirror
of tsx-11.mit.edu's kernel source code tree.  The daily mirror runs
start at 3am EST/EDT, though this may change from time to time.

In addition, this machine is the home of the Linux version/port of AIPS
(NRAO's massive Astronomical Image Processing System) and is (slowly!)
being set up as a primary site for the use of Linux in astronomy (and
associated scientific applications/fields).  Various things, probably
including partial mirrors of other major Linux sites, will be added as I
have the time to deal with them.  I will also make the site a little
more friendly in the near future (with ls-lR indexes, etc.).

The site is at NRAO's headquarters in Charlottesville, Virginia (USA),
on the campus of the University of Virginia.  We have good connectivity
to the Internet at large (fiber optic locally to a dual-T1 link), so
geographically close clients should get comfortable transfer speeds.

I've been running this site for a while now, rather quietly, and have
decided to make it known and available for public consumption as the
primary FTP sites just seem to be getting more and more loaded
(oftentimes refusing additional anonymous logins).

One note: I currently restrict the number of simultaneous anonymous
users here to a rather small number.  (Though I allow twice as many
between 2000 and 0800 right now--this is my desktop workstation for
running a large UNIX network of Suns, RS/6000's, etc., and I need to get
some work done during the day!)  I'm currently waiting on another 16MB
of RAM to come through our budget/procurement pipeline; this restriction
will be eased once I get that additional memory.

I am very open to suggestions regarding what else could be added to this
site, as well as to setting up an active incoming area (especially for
applications of an astronomical and/or scientific nature).

