This is the help file for 'linfo', a Linux Information Server.
Version 1.3, November 2, 1994

	This note announces an experimental Linux Information
Server (LINFO) with a mail interface. The rest of this post details
this service in a FAQ format.

1. About LINFO

1.0 What is LINFO ?

	LINFO stands for a Linux Information Server.
The database consists of posts to comp.os.linux.* starting from about
April of 1994.

1.1 What does LINFO do for me?

	You can ask questions and LINFO will try to give
you relevant answers. The comp.os.linux.* posts
contain a great deal of Linux problems and their solutions.
By using LINFO, you don't have to try to keep all Linux news
posts for reference. Anyway, many systems delete these after a
certain time, so they are not available.

	Additionally, LINFO has an easy to use search syntax
that allows you to find things more quickly than otherwise.

1.2 How much does LINFO cost?

	It's free to anyone that can send and receive mail
on the internet.

2. How to use LINFO

2.0 How do I use LINFO?

	You send mail to 'linfo@lorax.mv.com' [192.80.84.247]
with your request in the body of the mail message. After a while, you will
get a response back containing what LINFO found in its database.

2.1 What do the requests look like in the body of the mail message?

	At its simplest level, you just type the word 'dir'
followed by a word of what you want to find. For example,
if you wanted to ask LINFO about itself, you would type:

		dir linfo

	If you wanted to get a directory, and then have those files
returned to you, you would add a 'fetch' command as in:

		dir linfo
		fetch

	Notice that capitalization does not matter to LINFO. Searching
for topic LINFO is the same as searching for topic linfo.

	Soon, you will get back some mail with what LINFO found.

	There are lots of other things you can tell LINFO to
do. These are documented later.

2.2 Why don't I always receive an immediate reply?

	The LINFO server is not turned on all the time. Or, there
could be other people using it.

2.3 Why don't I receive a reply at all?

	It could be that LINFO can't reply to you via internet
mail for some reason. LINFO runs on Linux 1.1.47 with lots of
hacking, so sometimes it just can't reply. As I see these failures come
up, I try to remedy them, but I can't guarantee a reply.

2.3 Is there someone I can send mail to if I have a problem
    with LINFO?

	You can try sending me mail at:

	root@lorax.mv.com

	lorax.mv.com is internet address [192.80.84.247].

2.4 Sometimes I receive multiple news posts in one file I 
    retrieved. Why is this so?

	It is because LINFO combines news articles with the
same subject together as they are received from the internet.

3.0 Advanced Commands

3.1 What is the full set of commands that LINFO can execute?

	Here are the commands:

mailto your-account@node.com
----------------------------

	The 'mailto' command allows you to specify a different
reply for LINFO to use for requests.

	You can use 'replyto' in place of 'mailto' also.

help
----
	The 'help' command causes LINFO to send you this text back.

library name-list
-----------------

	The 'library' command restricts LINFO's search to a particular
library. Valid libraries at this time are:

	comp.os.linux.help
	comp.os.linux.misc
	comp.os.linux.announce
	comp.os.linux.admin
	comp.os.linux.development

	The command 'library    comp.os.linux.misc   comp.os.linux.help' will
search just the misc and help libraries.

	Here are some other libraries that might possibly
be up:

	alt.binaries.sounds.midi
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.cd-rom
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.chips
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.comm
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.misc
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.networking
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.storage
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.systems
	comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.video
	comp.windows.x.i386unix
	gnu.announce

	If you want me to add any others, they must be related
in some way to useful information about linux.

	You can also abbreviate the name of the library. The following
table gives you the abbreviations:

	Library				Abbreviation
	--------------------------------------------
	comp.os.linux.help		help
	comp.os.linux.misc		misc
	comp.os.linux.announce		announce
	comp.os.linux.admin		admin
	comp.os.linux.development	development

	Thus, the command 'library misc help' is equivalent to the
example above.

dir find-expression
--------------------

	The 'dir' command causes LINFO to search for news articles
matching 'find-expression' and return a directory of them.

	'find-expression' can be one of:

	some-string-with-no-spaces-in-it

		LINFO will search for a news article with 'some-string...'
		in the title.

	&&

		This is the boolean AND operator.

	||
		This is the boolean OR operator.

	!
		This is the boolean NOT operator.

	Additionally, 'find-verb' must be in RPN notation if you use
more that one 'some-string...' with the boolean operators. For example,
if you wanted to find out about mouse problems with the ATI graphics
card in X11, you would use this find command:

	dir mouse mice || ATI && X11 &&

	This command says: "Only find articles that have the string
                            ATI in the title, and the string X11 in
                            the title  and either the string
                            mouse or mice."

	Also, the find string is executed left to right.

	Forth-ers will recognize the syntax, as a forth engine
is used for the actual search. 'mouse' just pushes a true/false onto
the stack if it is found in the title. 'mice' does the same.
The '||' then or's the top two items on the stack, leaving the
result. 'ati' pushes its search results. The '&&' then ands
this result with the result from the '||'. Next, 'X11' pushes
its result, and finally, '&&' ands this with the previous information.
What is left on the stack is either TRUE or FALSE, and is returned
as the results of the search.

	The boolean operators must be bracketed by spaces. That
is, a 'dir egress!' is not the same as 'dir   egress   !'. The
first case will try to find the string 'egress!' in a title,
whereas the second will try not to find 'egress' in the title.

	You can construct some truly pathological find-expressions.
But, KISS, please !!!

	The return data is of the form:

	library/volume/file - Title

	You might receive something like:

	File Name                   Other Information
	-----------------------------------------------------
	comp.os.linux.help/1/22   - Date - Master Help Message
	comp.os.linux.help/5/322  - Date - Mice help needed !!!
	...

	Note if you do something like:

	dir ABC DEF GHI

	The forth engine will automatically '&&' any token values
together on its data stack before it returns to you. Therefore,
the above example becomes:

	dir ABC DEF GHI && &&

end
---
	This tells the mail processor to end your session and return
any information to you. You should use it if you attach .signatures
or other data to the end of your mail messages so you won't get
the help file returned to you once the mail processor encounters
the .signature.

fetch
-----

	This command fetches each file that would found during
a successful 'dir' operation above.

get library/volume/file
-----------------------

	This allows you to get an individual file. To get file
'comp.os.linux.help/1/22', you would enter:

	get comp.os.linux.help/1/22

limit DDD
---------

	This limits the number of bytes you can have returned to
DDD bytes. By default, this is set to 45000 if you don't specify
a 'limit'. If you set a higher limit, and LINFO finds more than
45000 bytes of data, it will send you multiple mail messages.

	The limit is checked after each file is appended to your
message. Therefore, you could receive more than the limit
you specify.

	Additionally, Linfo will return only 40000 bytes or
so per mail message. There is no way to currently change this
parameter.

after dd-mm-yy
--------------

	Only return information that was published after dd-mm-yy.
The date can only be in this format, ie: dd is between 1 and 31,
the month can be ONLY 'Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Oct Nov Dec',
and the year can be 94 and later.

comment any-comment-text
------------------------

	Anything proceeded by 'comment' is treated as a comment.
It is a way to send the human operator a message about LINFO,
as I scan for these from time to time.


3.2 Advanced Commands Example
-----------------------------

	Here is an example using the advanced commands to
ask for up to 100,000 bytes of data from the 'admin' library
that have both 'afio' and ('scsi' or 'qic') in their subject. [afio
is a backup recovery utility, and you are probably interested in using it
on a scsi bus with a QIC tape drive] Nothing will be returned
before the first of July, 1994. The results of the search will be
returned to 'me@mynode.com' with the most recently found articles first.

	after      01-Jul-94
	limit      100000
	library    comp.os.linux.admin
	replyto    me@mynode.com
	dir        scsi qic || afio &&
	fetch
	end

4.0 About LINFO and its Goals
-----------------------------

	LINFO is provided as a public service to the Linux Internet
Community by compuPage, Inc., a New Hampshire, USA Corporation.
The corporation does include a small advertisement for its services
in all LINFO replies. If you object to this, don't send mail
to LINFO! Or, you can just write yourself a mail filter to delete
the add so you don't have to see it.

	The goal of LINFO is to make Linux easier for the
inexperienced user to use.

	The mail interface was chosen over ftp or gopher or
WWW, as it is more universal, does not require an active
TCP connection to operate, and can be batched and staged at
various points along the process.

	The node, 'lorax.mv.com', is a PC-AT 386-25 clone
with an Adaptec 1542 SCSI controller with various disks. It
is connected to the internet via a 14.4 dial up slip line
to MV Communications of NH. It is currently running Linux 1.1.47,
with modifications.

	The database itself is just a flat file consisting of
a series of compressed news feed articles. The search algorithm is
linear using a forth engine to perform matches.

Appendix I - Definitions
------------------------

RPN - Reverse Polish Notation

	A method of specifying mathematical operations named
	after a famous Polish Mathematician.

KISS - Keep it Simple Stupid

	Used to remind others to keep things simple and easy to use.

LINFO - The Linux Information Server at linfo@lorax.mv.com.

QIC - Quarter Inch Cartridge

	A name of a type of computer tape drive.

Appendix II - Known Bugs and Things To Do
------------------------------------------

1. Which news groups to add?

	I would like to find a way for linfo to read and
discard certain news that has no relation to linux. In this
way, I could increase the number of news groups scanned, and
still keep the disk usage down to a minimum.

	For example, any user could request that a certain
news group be followed, and articles be kept based on criteria.
One criteria might be keywords.

	The syntax might resemble:

	Follow	somegroup.news.misc.bla.bla with keywords
	linux gnu X11.

	Any ideas?

Appendix III - Notes
--------------------

1)
	Because the mailer linfo uses tries to process
and expand any '\n~XXX' commands, linfo will either remove
or modify these sequences in the data stream. At times,
it may just eliminate the '~', but the newer software will
add a space between the '\n' and the '~'.

2)
	I see most people don't read the manual, or don't remember
it long enough to send in a valid request. Here are the most
common errors:

	2.1 You want to find out about 'modem' AND 'setup'
	    so you send this request:

		'dir modem && setup'

	    WRONG! Its RPN. Go re-read the manual. You
	    should have sent:

		'dir modem setup &&'

	    Note if you also sent:

		'dir modem setup'

	    you would be OK too, as linfo appends the '&&' automatically
	    for you.

	2.2 You want to find out about 'modem' and 'setup'
	    so you ask for a directory, but not to have
	    the data files returned. For example, if
	    you said:

	        limit 100000
		'dir modem setup &&'

	    You should have said:

	        limit 100000
		'dir modem setup &&'
		fetch	


