This is the Linux/MIPS FAQ. If you can't find an answer to
your question here, please contact <linux@waldorf-gmbh.de>.
The current version of this FAQ is always ftpable from
"ftp.waldorf-gmbh.de:/pub/linux/mips/linux-mips-FAQ" and
also available as a WWW page
"http://www.waldorf-gmbh.de:/linux-mips-faq.html".

Last update: 94/11/09


Q) What is "Linux/MIPS" ?

A) Linux/MIPS is a Linux port for computers equipped with
   Mips R4x00 processors. R3000 systems might be supported
   too. For further information, see below.


Q) On what hardware will Linux/MIPS run ?

A) Our first target platform is the Deskstation Tyne series.
   These systems support R4400PC and R4600 processors with
   speeds currently up to 133 MHz. The main boards are equipped
   with up to 2 Meg L2 Cache, 256 Meg main memory (PS/2 SIMMs)
   and have 4 ISA plus 2 VLB slots.

   For further information about Tyne computers, in particular
   prices, please contact Deskstation Technology at
   800-793-3375 (US only), ++1-913-599-1900 (international) or
   ++44-773-829-542 (Europe).

   In the meantime many other platforms turned out to be
   good candidates for Linux/MIPS. Although we don't have
   code for Mips platforms other than the Deskstation systems
   yet, chances are quite good that some of the following
   systems will be supported in future:

   - All ARC (ARC = Advanced Risc Computing) compliant Mips
     machines, such as Mips Magnum 4000PC and SC,
     ShaBLAMM! Nitro-VLB, systems based on the Acer PICA1
     design and probably many others.

   - Mips Magnum 3000
   - Mips RC3230
   - DECstation 3100 (with R3000 cpu)
   - Sony News (with R3000 cpu)
 

Q) Is Linux/MIPS little or big endian ?

A) At least all of the R4x00 ports will be little endian since
   many of the newer Mips platforms don't support big endian.
   In contrast to this, it might be necessary to run older
   Mips systems, such as the Sony News, in big endian mode.
   If and how we can provide user code compatibility thru the
   whole Mips line hasn't been decided yet.


Q) Is Linux/MIPS a 64 bit OS ?

A) No, and for a simple reason: Linux/MIPS is based on the
   latest Linux/i386 sources. As long Linux/i386 is a 32bit
   system Linux/MIPS will it be too.


Q) What is the state of the project ?

A) We have a halfway working kernel for the Deskstation boards.
   Console, floppy, serial and parallel I/O seem to be OK. The
   C library is nearly complete. We expect the first user process
   running soon and in so far this FAQ is a progress report!


Q) Any support/development tools available ?

A) Yes. We have cross compilers, assemblers and linkers ready
   to use for Linux/i386, SunOs 4.1.3 and Solaris2.3.
   A Mips R2000/R3000 simulator (SPIM) for Linux/i386 is also
   ready to download. Binaries and documentation are available
   from following ftp sites:

   sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/ALPHA/mips	
   ftp.uni-mainz.de:/pub/Linux/MIPS
   ftp.waldorf-gmbh.de:/pub/linux/mips

   There's also a large number of useful docs about Mips
   processors on sgigate.sgi.com:/pub/doc.


Q) Is there a mailing list ?

A) Yes. We created a linux-activists channel called "mips".
   To subscribe to this list, send a message to
   <linux-activists-request@niksula.hut.fi> with the command
   "X-Mn-Admin: join mips" as the first and only line.


Q) Can I help ?

A) Sure! If you have a Mips box, please let us know. Eventually
   we find a way to include your box to the target list. And
   we would of course appreciate if you can spend some time
