Version 2.01 of the new (BETA) Linux (E)IDE driver is now released for use.

The latest version incorporates several minor fixes to speed up boot-time
probing and eliminate extraneous kernel messages.  Courtesy of Scott Snyder,
the (E)IDE driver now supports IDE cd-rom drives.  The NEC-260 cd-rom drive
is known to work (with blocksize=2048), and others probabily will as well.

The only change from version 2.0 is that irq-unmasking is OFF again by default.

The new (BETA) Linux (E)IDE driver differs from the stock hd.c as follows:

	- support for dual IDE interfaces, on any IRQs.
	- automatic probing for interfaces and IRQs.
	- a single IRQ can be shared between two interfaces.
	- automatic detection/identification of up to four IDE devices.
	- enhanced command line parameters to supplement auto-probing.
	- transparent LBA (Linear Block Addressing) on drives which support it.
	- support for IDE cd-rom drive(s) on either/both interface(s).
	- support for "standby" power-saving modes.
	- faster, more streamlined driver code to reduce driver CPU cycles.
	- more accurate timeouts, instead of just looping 100000 times.
	- support for setting EIDE devices to use PIO-mode3 on a jumpered i/f.

The advanced features of the stock and enhanced drivers can be tuned using
the hdparm utility, currently at version 1.9.

The latest versions are always available from sunsite.unc.edu
in /pub/Linux/Incoming or /pub/Linux/kernel/patches/diskdrives
and from tsx-11.mit.edu in /pub/linux/patches.

Look for:	ide-2.01.patch.61+.gz	(driver for kernel 1.1.61 and higher)
		ide-x.x.patch.lsm	(lsm entry)
		hdparm-1.8.tar.gz	(current version of the hdparm utility)
		hdparm-1.9.tar.gz	(coming soon)

This product is currently a joint effort of:

	mlord@bnr.ca			(Mark Lord)
	delman@mipg.upenn.edu		(Delman Lee)
	ptjmatti@kruuna.helsinki.fi	(Petri Mattila)
	snyder@fnald0.fnal.gov		(Scott Snyder)

Anyone who can contribute specialized knowledge of specific enhanced
IDE interface cards is welcome and encouraged to join the team.


