	The Adaptec acb-40XX is a SCSI <---> MFM/RLL disk  bridge board. 
The acb-4000 is the MFM version (18 sectors/cyl) and the acb-4070 is the
RLL version (26 sectors/cyl). These are old SCSI-I devices that don't
support the SCSI INQUIRY command. The Linux kernel uses this command
to detect devices at boot, and hence these old devices will get ignored.

But that is only half the problem. The other part is that you need to
know the vendor-specific MODE_SELECT parameters used to configure the
drive geometry and such before you can low-level format and use the
board with an old MFM/RLL drive.

	I have packaged up a small kernel patch to take care of 
the first problem, and written a small "format" utility to take care
of the second problem. More details can be found in the README
file that comes with the package.

	So anyone with an old MFM/RLL hard disk and an acb-40XX bridge
board (commonly found in old late 80's 3Com file servers) can plug it
onto their SCSI bus and have a bit more hard disk space.

Here is the LSM. Look for it in ./Incoming for now, on sunsite.

Paul.

Begin3
Title:          Adaptec acb-40XX SCSI <---> MFM/RLL bridge board kit.
Version:        1.00
Entered-date:   Nov 8, 1994
