After my recent post to c.o.l.announce about NQS, I've received mail from a
number of people asking me to explain what NQS is.

The Network Queueing System (NQS)
---------------------------------

Users place their work into a queue, as they would a printout.  NQS takes
the work out of the queue, and runs it on a suitable machine :

  o  Clusters of workstations together add up to a significant amount of
     processing power, but are often idle, for example overnight.  NQS can
     be used to run jobs during these idle periods, so making better use of
     resources.
     
  o  Large computational machine are often wasting time because too many 
     people are trying to run large processes at the same time.  NQS can
     limit the number of concurrent jobs, so improving overall throughput.
     
Limits can be placed on the resources that any submitted job can use, such
as the amount of CPU time, or memory usage.  You can also restrict how many
jobs can be run at a time on any given machine, allowing you to also
maintain usable interactive service.

---

I should also point out that the original port to Linux was done by Karsten
Steffens, and not Karsten Steffan as I originally said.

Stuart
