Current version: 1.41
Release date: Fri Nov 11 08:07:22 EST 1994

What's new since 1.40.3
-----------------------

1. xqddb(1) is now multi-user
2. qstall(1) will not stabilize if any Qddb application
   is running on the relation.   New option '-wait' forces 
   qstall(1) to wait until all applications have closed
   the relation.   Applications cannot be started on the
   relation until it is finished.
3. Configuration changes, now uses autoconf-2.1
4. Performance enhancements
5. Interface bug fixes and enhancements
6. Record locking now works properly under Linux
7. Printing of search results and instance views
8. New example program, qhelp, is a hypertext help system
   using Qddb as the storage mechanism.  (requires BLT)

Qddb is a database suite that allows you to create relations,
add tuples, modify tuples, delete tuples, and search for tuples
in a fast and very flexible way.   Qddb supports the following
search techniques: regular expressions, words and word ranges, 
numbers and numeric ranges, and dates and date ranges.  Qddb 1.41 
can use Tcl as its configuration language, so you can build custom 
interfaces to your Qddb databases with it.   We provide a reasonably 
nice generic interface so you can be up and running quickly.   There 
are manual pages for all the utilities and Tcl commands, and we 
include draft 0 (basically the "getting started" section) of a 
user's manual.

Qddb 1.41 can be obtained from:
	ftp.ms.uky.edu:/pub/unix/qddb-1.41.tar.gz
	ftp.ms.uky.edu:/pub/unix/qddb-examples.tar.gz 
        ftp.ms.uky.edu:/pub/unix/qhelp-0.3.tar.gz

If you choose to use the Tcl/Tk interface, you'll need the
latest versions of both (Tcl 7.3 and Tk 3.6p1).

Read the FAQ and README; the user's manual in qddb-1.41/Doc/Manual 
has enough info to get you started.

The qddb-examples.tar.gz file contains a few example relations, one
being a fully populated "Jargon File" relation.  (Nothing new
since qddb-1.40.3).

The qhelp-0.3.tar.gz is an example hypertext help system that 
uses Qddb as the mechanism for storing hypertext link information.

This is BETA software, but we have been happily using the underlying
stuff for years.  The purpose for this release is to get feedback
from people running different systems.  It is not a general-purpose 
extension to Tcl/Tk, but an application that uses Tcl/Tk as its 
configuration language.

We have tested Qddb on MIPS Ultrix, OSF/1 (Alpha), BSD/386, Linux, SunOS
and Solaris.   We have reports that it works under UnixWare, FreeBSD,
and NetBSD.  If you have a different operating system or architecture,
we'd appreciate hearing from you.

*****    From the README    *****

QDDB stands for 'Quick and Dirty DataBase' (due to the nature of its origin) 
and is rapidly evolving.  Most of the code has changed drastically (or has 
been totally rewritten) from the original version and is now much more 
stable.  The program(s) are currently in BETA condition, so there are 
undoubtedly numerous bugs that we would appreciate hearing about.  Suggestions
and bug reports are also quite welcome.

There is a mailing list 'qddb-users@ms.uky.edu' that you can 
subscribe to for general discussion/information about qddb.  Send mail to:

	'qddb-users-request@ms.uky.edu'

with the subject of 'subscribe' and a body containing your name/e-mail address.
I monitor this list and I will usually answer posted questions.  For bug 
reports, you may wish to submit them to both 'qddb-users@ms.uky.edu'
and 'qddb-bugs@ms.uky.edu' so that other qddb users may benefit from your
ill experiences.


