Keywords: CDU31A, Sony, device driver, autoprobing

Because the Sony interface cards have no identifying features other
than 4 I/O locations, a command must be issued to them to verify their
presence.  Unfortunately, this can be very destructive to the rest of
the system if another device happens to sit at the test location.
Many people were having problems with this messing up their
system. For that reason, all autoprobes for the CDU31A driver have
been removed as of 1.1.64.

NOTE: THIS MEANS THE CDU31A DRIVER WILL NOT WORK UNTIL YOU SET A
      CONFIG FOR IT.

To make it work, you need to either add your drive to the config table
in the driver or use a LILO boot-line configuration.  The LILO boot
config is:

	cdu31a=<I/O address>,<interrupt>[,PAS]

Setting the interrupt to 0 will disable the interrupts and use polled I/O.
The PAS is optionally for PAS-16 users that are not using the sound driver.

A normal card would have a config like:

	cdu31a=0x340,0

Using interrupt 5, use:

	cdu31a=0x340,5

You can either type this in at LILO boot (tedious) or add it to the
lilo.conf file.  For instance, I have the following at the top of
my lilo.conf file:

	append="cdu31a=0x1f88,0,PAS"

I highly recommend the LILO method, it works well and is flexible.

Please read this, tell your friends, tell your enemies, etc.  I don't
want a million people posting to the newsgroups or mailing me saying:
"My CDU31A stopped working in 1.1.64.  Why?"

